  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 256 AM MST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nAn unusual upper-level flow pattern is expected late Sunday as a \ntrough exits the central CONUS. Ensemble guidance projects \nmeridional flow aloft for the County Warning Area (CWA) as a ridge \nslides in from the northern Rockies. Most model runs are now \nanticipating this building ridge to propagate faster to the east \nthan prior runs. Simultaneously, the trough seems to slow down a bit \nas it makes its way eastward across the southern plains. Northeast \nupper-level winds should result as the exiting trough becomes more \npositively tilted than originally thought. Moving on to Monday, said \nridge finally pushes the resisting trough away and the system is \nable to proceed with it's eastward propagation across our region. \nInitially, northerly flow aloft is expected Monday transitioning \nbriefly to more of a zonal look early Tuesday as the ridge axis \npasses over the CWA. Weak southwest flow is then forecasted later in \nthe day Tuesday, with zonal flow once again taking over shortly \nafter. Guidance is now starting to lock onto a stronger ridge system \nbeginning to influence our coverage area on Wednesday, ushering in \nnorthwest winds at the 300-500mb level. \n\n\nOne may wonder what is to come from this peculiar synoptic setup \nacross the Tri-State area. The answer is likely nothing but dry \nconditions and seasonably pleasant temperatures as high pressure is \nset to take control both higher up in the atmosphere and at the \nsurface. Furthermore, moisture content will be quite limited as \ndewpoints in the 30s/40s and Relative Humidity (RH) values ranging \n25-55% are forecast. Surface analysis does anticipate three frontal \nboundaries to move through during this forecast period (one each on \nSunday, Tuesday and Wednesday) but dry air should limit any chance \nfor precipitation. Therefore, rain will be highly unlikely as \nProbability of Precipitation fall below 5% for the entire long-term \nforecast period. \n\n\nHigh temperatures, as mentioned above, should be quite pleasant for \nthis time of the year across the CWA. Daily highs in the 60s will be \ncommonplace Sunday through the middle of next week. Overnight lows \nshould follow a slight warming trend. Low temperatures at or above \nfreezing on Sunday morning likely increases to upper 30s/low 40s \nMonday morning through Wednesday morning. Some locales may not even \nsee morning temps fall below 42 degrees on Wednesday. It is not \nlikely to be very windy at all going into next week as guidance \noutlines general 5-15 mph winds Sunday through Wednesday with some \nwindier prone areas of eastern Colorado potentially seeing up to 20 \nmph sustained winds. Sunday likely is the windiest day but gusts \nshouldn't exceed 25 mph. RH values may dip into the low 20s \nSunday/Monday for areas at or west of US-385 and 20-30% for \neverywhere in the CWA east of there but dry fuels are not \nanticipated so fire weather chances will be low. Sunday will be the \nonly day to monitor for that but again, there's a very low \nchance of even elevated fire weather occurring in eastern \nColorado.",
